In orders_2nf, cust_email → customer, cust_city, cust_country: those depend on the customer, not on the order. In order_lines_2nf, supplier → supplier_tel. Both are non-key columns determining other non-key columns.
CREATE TABLE customers (
customer_id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
email citext NOT NULL UNIQUE,
name text NOT NULL,
city text,
country char(2) NOT NULL REFERENCES countries(code)
);
CREATE TABLE suppliers (
supplier_id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
name text NOT NULL UNIQUE,
phone text
);
CREATE TABLE products (
sku text PRIMARY KEY,
name text NOT NULL,
supplier_id bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES suppliers
);
CREATE TABLE orders (
order_id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
customer_id bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES customers,
ordered_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);
CREATE TABLE order_lines (
order_id bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES orders ON DELETE CASCADE,
line_no int NOT NULL,
sku text NOT NULL REFERENCES products,
qty int NOT NULL CHECK (qty > 0),
unit_price numeric(12,2) NOT NULL CHECK (unit_price >= 0),
PRIMARY KEY (order_id, line_no)
);
3NF eliminates: the update anomaly where changing a supplier's phone number requires touching every order line they ever appeared on.
Note unit_price stays on order_lines even though products have prices. That is not a normalization failure — the price at the time of sale is a genuinely different fact from the current catalogue price. Ask "does this value depend on the key, or on the moment?" before deleting a column that looks redundant.

If you cannot, you have not denormalized — you have introduced a bug with a schedule.
-- A cached counter kept honest by a trigger
ALTER TABLE posts ADD COLUMN comment_count int NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;
CREATE FUNCTION bump_comment_count() RETURNS trigger LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
BEGIN
IF TG_OP = 'INSERT' THEN
UPDATE posts SET comment_count = comment_count + 1 WHERE id = NEW.post_id;
ELSIF TG_OP = 'DELETE' THEN
UPDATE posts SET comment_count = comment_count - 1 WHERE id = OLD.post_id;
END IF;
RETURN NULL;
END $$;
CREATE TRIGGER comments_count_trg
AFTER INSERT OR DELETE ON comments
FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION bump_comment_count();
Watch out: this trigger serialises all comment inserts for a single hot post onto one row lock. For a viral post that is a contention bottleneck. The alternative is an append-only delta table summed periodically — a trade of freshness for throughput.

CREATE TABLE post_tags (
post_id bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES posts ON DELETE CASCADE,
tag_id int NOT NULL REFERENCES tags ON DELETE CASCADE,
added_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
PRIMARY KEY (post_id, tag_id)
);
-- The PK covers (post_id, tag_id). Add the mirror index for the other direction:
CREATE INDEX ON post_tags (tag_id, post_id);
The composite primary key does double duty: it enforces "a tag can only be applied once" and serves as the access path from post to tags. The second index serves tag to posts. Add a surrogate key to a join table only when something else must reference the relationship itself.

-- Default GIN (jsonb_ops): supports @>, ?, ?|, ?&, @?, @@. Larger index.
CREATE INDEX events_payload_gin ON events USING gin (payload);
-- jsonb_path_ops: supports ONLY @> (and jsonpath equality). Smaller and faster.
CREATE INDEX events_payload_gin_path ON events USING gin (payload jsonb_path_ops);
-- Expression btree: for one hot scalar key, this beats GIN outright.
CREATE INDEX events_campaign ON events ((payload ->> 'campaign'));
-- Partial + expression: narrowest and cheapest of all
CREATE INDEX events_order_total ON events (((payload ->> 'total')::numeric))
WHERE kind = 'order';
Choosing: if you query a known key with equality or range, use an expression btree index. If you query arbitrary keys with containment, use GIN — jsonb_path_ops when @> is all you need, default jsonb_ops when you also need key-existence operators.
Common pitfall: payload ->> 'total' > '100' compares text, so '99' > '100' is true. Cast explicitly — (payload ->> 'total')::numeric > 100 — and make sure your index uses the identical expression, cast included, or it will not be used.

Split every breaking change into phases that are individually backwards-compatible:
Expand — add the new structure. Nullable column, new table, new index built CONCURRENTLY. Old code keeps working.
Backfill — populate the new structure in batches, committing as you go so no single transaction holds a snapshot for hours.
Dual-write — deploy application code that writes both old and new. Reads still come from old.
Migrate reads — deploy code that reads from new. Verify.
Contract — stop writing the old structure, then drop it, once you are certain no rollback will need it.
Renaming a column becomes: add new_name → backfill → dual-write → switch reads → drop old_name. Five deployments instead of one, and zero downtime at every step.
-- Backfill in bounded batches, never one giant UPDATE
DO $$
DECLARE rows_done int;
BEGIN
LOOP
UPDATE users SET email_normalised = lower(email)
WHERE id IN (
SELECT id FROM users WHERE email_normalised IS NULL
ORDER BY id LIMIT 5000 FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED
);
GET DIAGNOSTICS rows_done = ROW_COUNT;
EXIT WHEN rows_done = 0;
COMMIT; -- requires a procedure or a client-side loop
END LOOP;
END $$;

Adding a plain column with a default has been non-rewriting since PostgreSQL 11 — the default is recorded in the catalogue and materialised lazily. That makes this fast even on a huge table:
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN currency char(3) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'JPY'; -- PG 11+: no rewrite
A volatile default still rewrites the whole table:
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN token uuid NOT NULL DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(); -- FULL REWRITE
For that case, and for adding NOT NULL to an existing column, use the constraint-first route:
-- 1. Add nullable, no default. Instant.
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN token uuid;
-- 2. Backfill in batches (see above), and have the application populate new rows.
-- 3. Add a NOT VALID check: takes a brief lock, validates nothing yet.
ALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT orders_token_nn CHECK (token IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID;
-- 4. Validate: takes only SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE, so reads and writes continue.
ALTER TABLE orders VALIDATE CONSTRAINT orders_token_nn;
-- 5. PostgreSQL 12+ can now prove NOT NULL from the validated CHECK without a full scan.
ALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN token SET NOT NULL;
ALTER TABLE orders DROP CONSTRAINT orders_token_nn;
The same NOT VALID → VALIDATE CONSTRAINT two-step works for foreign keys, and it is the only sane way to add one to a large table.

Given a table (student, course, instructor) where each instructor teaches exactly one course, list the functional dependencies, show why it is in 3NF but not BCNF, and give the decomposition.
Solution
-- FDs: (student, course) -> instructor ; instructor -> course
-- Candidate keys: (student, course) and (student, instructor)
-- `instructor` is a determinant but not a candidate key -> BCNF violation.
CREATE TABLE teaches (
instructor text PRIMARY KEY,
course text NOT NULL
);
CREATE TABLE enrolment (
student_id int NOT NULL REFERENCES students,
instructor text NOT NULL REFERENCES teaches,
PRIMARY KEY (student_id, instructor)
);
